1
00:00:00,270 --> 00:00:04,106
A partial differential equation can produce a beautiful animation.

2
00:00:04,110 --> 00:00:06,795
But what makes that picture useful for research?

3
00:00:06,990 --> 00:00:10,742
We need to see the model, check the calculation, and keep the evidence.

4
00:00:11,050 --> 00:00:15,773
This is PhaseAtlas: a visual workbench that connects those steps.

5
00:00:17,270 --> 00:00:20,457
Start with an equation and the question you want to investigate.

6
00:00:20,590 --> 00:00:25,212
In the Studio, researchers can edit the model, initial data, and boundary conditions.

7
00:00:25,770 --> 00:00:29,683
Supported models include coupled evolution equations and fourth-order problems.

8
00:00:29,830 --> 00:00:35,245
The numerical method and its limits stay visible, so users can choose an appropriate experiment.

9
00:00:36,270 --> 00:00:39,760
Here is a practical example: a hot rod losing heat through its ends.

10
00:00:39,790 --> 00:00:42,720
The Robin boundary condition models exchange with the surroundings.

11
00:00:42,930 --> 00:00:45,870
Watch the temperature fall, while the centre remains warmer than the edges.

12
00:00:45,870 --> 00:00:48,015
Every frame comes from a numerical solution.

13
00:00:48,110 --> 00:00:50,690
We can measure the mean temperature or follow a local probe.

14
00:00:50,690 --> 00:00:53,487
This dimensionless example helps explore heat transfer;

15
00:00:53,530 --> 00:00:57,085
a prediction for a specific material would also require calibration.

16
00:00:58,270 --> 00:01:00,785
A convincing animation is only the first step.

17
00:01:00,850 --> 00:01:04,759
We test the boundary implementation against a heat-equation solution that is known exactly.

18
00:01:04,830 --> 00:01:08,530
Keeping the time step fixed, we refine the grid from sixteen to thirty-two to sixty-four

19
00:01:08,530 --> 00:01:12,357
cells. The errors decrease, with an observed spatial order close to two.

20
00:01:12,430 --> 00:01:17,110
A separate balance diagnostic compares the change in thermal content with the recorded heat input

21
00:01:17,110 --> 00:01:18,464
through the boundaries.

22
00:01:19,270 --> 00:01:21,346
Next, compare a family of experiments.

23
00:01:21,530 --> 00:01:24,990
This map asks how much heat remains after a fixed time, as diffusion and boundary

24
00:01:24,990 --> 00:01:30,180
exchange change. Every cell represents a completed numerical trajectory that can be replayed.

25
00:01:30,350 --> 00:01:32,497
Researchers can select their own observables.

26
00:01:32,690 --> 00:01:37,330
Failed runs and unobserved threshold events remain explicit, so the map does not silently hide

27
00:01:37,330 --> 00:01:38,807
missing evidence.

28
00:01:40,270 --> 00:01:44,430
For fourth-order equations, we can inspect a wave as a three-dimensional surface across space and

29
00:01:44,430 --> 00:01:48,610
time. Rotate the view, follow a selected time slice, and compare changes from the initial

30
00:01:48,610 --> 00:01:53,050
state or a reference formula. The same saved trajectory drives the animation and the numerical

31
00:01:53,050 --> 00:01:57,715
diagnostics. Global, local, and weighted norms help study wave motion and deformation.

32
00:01:57,970 --> 00:02:01,201
Each endpoint can have two custom linear boundary conditions.

33
00:02:02,270 --> 00:02:04,161
The result is more than an animation.

34
00:02:04,630 --> 00:02:09,814
Researchers can export the model, parameters, trajectories, and numerical checks, alongside figures and video.

35
00:02:10,250 --> 00:02:13,464
Selected problems can be compared with an independent Python implementation.

36
00:02:13,770 --> 00:02:15,917
Computation runs on the user's device.

37
00:02:15,930 --> 00:02:20,130
That makes the workflow accessible for exploring a model, checking an idea, and sharing a

38
00:02:20,130 --> 00:02:22,091
reproducible calculation.

39
00:02:23,270 --> 00:02:25,090
Here is the workflow in the live Studio.

40
00:02:25,250 --> 00:02:28,244
Start from a heat-transfer model, then edit the equation and its parameters.

41
00:02:28,390 --> 00:02:31,070
Type the spatial derivatives directly into the equation box.

42
00:02:34,170 --> 00:02:37,710
I will use diffusion on a one-dimensional rod, with heat exchange at both ends.

43
00:02:37,730 --> 00:02:41,070
Each boundary has its own coefficients, so we can change the environment without rewriting the

44
00:02:41,070 --> 00:02:44,385
solver. The left and right ends can also use different boundary data.

45
00:02:48,090 --> 00:02:52,190
Now run the experiment. Play the saved solution, pause it, or scrub through time to

46
00:02:52,190 --> 00:02:59,658
inspect a particular frame. Then choose Check resolution.

47
00:02:59,890 --> 00:03:02,970
The platform reruns the model with a smaller time step and a finer grid, and

48
00:03:02,970 --> 00:03:06,602
reports the differences. This comparison is something we can actually check.

49
00:03:09,250 --> 00:03:11,485
Finally, export the experiment as JSON.

50
00:03:11,630 --> 00:03:15,830
It includes the configuration, the saved fields, and the numerical checks, so another researcher can

51
00:03:15,830 --> 00:03:18,099
inspect and reproduce the calculation.

52
00:03:23,270 --> 00:03:28,710
The longer-term goal is a research platform that helps people ask better mathematical questions and

53
00:03:28,710 --> 00:03:33,377
inspect the evidence together. I am Niu Te from Imperial College London.

54
00:03:33,670 --> 00:03:35,929
This is PhaseAtlas.
